{
  "image_name": "ccbdb9ccb1529b6b19d9b53761595bee.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure gives an overview of biogeochemical processes in a flooded wetland or rice-field system, linking the atmosphere, water, plants, soil, microbes, and animals. The central plant is shown as a key mediator: it takes up CO2 for photosynthesis, releases O2 to the root zone, and transports gases such as CH4 and N2O through internal air spaces (aerenchyma) from soil to atmosphere. Methane also reaches the air by diffusion and bubbling, while some CH4 is oxidized to CO2 in more oxygenated zones.\n\nThe soil is divided into aerobic and anaerobic regions, highlighting how oxygen availability controls different transformations. In anaerobic soil, decomposing organic matter supports methanogenesis, respiration, and carbon cycling. Around the roots, the rhizosphere receives root exudates and oxygen, stimulating microorganisms and affecting nutrient availability and plant uptake. Mineral and organic phosphorus are connected by biological and biochemical mineralization, with adsorption onto substrates also indicated.\n\nOn the right, nitrogen cycling is summarized: organic N is converted to NH4+-N, then to NO2−-N and NO3−-N through nitrification, with pathways for denitrification, DNRA, anammox, volatilization of NH3, and emissions of N2O and NH3. Overall, the figure emphasizes coupled carbon, nitrogen, and phosphorus cycling in flooded soils.",
